Z||000000||-=shame=-||03-19-2001||04:36 PM||||No it hasn't burnt yet. I managed to smell the burning plastic first, I'm very scared. My Fiero is my only means of transportation and I can't afford to lose it. Please help. Here we go.<BR>'86 GT V-6 Automatic (if that matters) it feels as if exhaust is leaking in the front towards the fire wall. The burning plastic smell was (were) the wires that go through the fire wall. Whats down there that could be leaking? Yes manifold but it doesn't feel that way (not that I would know what it is supposed to feel like.) I can't feel or see any cracks or holes. Could this be a missing sensor, bolt, something easy? <BR>I'm not getting any codes but half those wires are charcoal now so I might not ever get a code.<BR>I'M TERRIFIED! <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||03-19-2001||05:22 PM||||Does it sound any different?  There are a lot of exhaust items in that area.  Manifolds, crossover connector pipe, EGR pipe, head pipe and the catalytic converter. Any one of these could be leaking but you should hear something coming from somewhere.  Use a short rubber hose held to your ear and move it around to find the leak.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-19-2001||07:15 PM||||You should be scared.<P>First, disconnect the battery and fix all the wires.<P>then find out what is cooking them.<P>BTW... Those repairs need to be done with marine crimps or weatherproof heatshink. Self vulcanizing tape will also work if used right. Electrical tape, normal heatshrink, or standard insulated crimps will not do. (That is unless you want to be doing this agian in a couple months because something corroded.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||03-19-2001||09:48 PM||||Shouldnt the big wiring harness that goes through the firewall (the one coming from C500 by the fusible links by battery), be wrapped in heat reflective/protective tape as part of the recall?  I know mine was (I had to take it off to check for a short in the loom).<P><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sevaan||03-19-2001||10:03 PM||||Check and make sure the spark plug wires are not sagging and touching anything behind it. One of my wires was doing that and the plastic melted.<p>[This message has been edited by Sevaan (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tweek||03-19-2001||11:33 PM||||Do you smoke?||||reg||1
Z||000006||-=shame=-||03-20-2001||04:32 PM||||Alright, as best as I can tell all the wiring is still good. Ugly but good. The heat seems to be coming from the forward exhaust manifold. This makes some sense because when I replaced the dogbone it may have taken some pressure off of the 15 year old exhaust gasket. <BR>Now I'm hunting down a mechanic that will do this work. I've been told no twice already because of the work envolved in a broken bolt. I know someone might say "What, you can't do it yourself?"<BR>Grandpa said "Being good at fixing things yourself begins with knowing what you can't do."||||reg||1
